Exact fifteen-message cap succeeds with leader 3; stored individual messages: 0.
// result_end

The fixed-snapshot counts here do not establish behavior under crashes during
an election, false suspicions, partitions, recovery, retries or simultaneous
independent election epochs. The three-message abstraction follows the common
teaching presentation in [Krzyzanowski's Rutgers notes](https://people.cs.rutgers.edu/~pxk/classes/417/notes/mutex.html).
[Garcia-Molina's original paper](https://homepage.divms.uiowa.edu/~ghosh/Bully.pdf)
contains additional halt, reorganization and ready procedures, plus explicit
failure assumptions. Those mechanisms are not claimed by either datamunge API.
A logical send index or a candidate-phase index cannot be read as a latency
bound without a timed delivery and timeout model.

Native tests exhaust all 9,217 live-initiator choices across all liveness masks
through ten processes, plus empty/all-down controls. They compare both ordinary
and traced APIs with closed counts, exact message/category/delivery records and
final learned leaders. An independent queued-message simulator delays answers
and activations while checking full-model outcomes; 1,000 larger random masks
exercise holes and non-Boolean liveness bytes. Budget boundaries, ignored tail
flags, malformed inputs, id capacity, existing distributed tests, formatting and
UBSan checks cover the remaining contracts. No dataset is needed for this
protocol-mechanism report; all inputs are explicit or generated logical systems.

> References

- H. Garcia-Molina (1982), [Elections in a Distributed Computing System](https://homepage.divms.uiowa.edu/~ghosh/Bully.pdf),
  IEEE Transactions on Computers C-31(1), 48–59. Section III and Appendix I describe
  the original bully algorithm and its broader reorganization/failure setting.
- P. Krzyzanowski, [Distributed Mutual Exclusion and Leader Election](https://people.cs.rutgers.edu/~pxk/classes/417/notes/mutex.html),
  Rutgers CS 417 lecture notes, for the ELECTION/OK/COORDINATOR teaching model and
  once-per-election activation of live responders.
